    "result_text": "The MapStruct mapper auto-maps the new fields in both directions. The implementation is complete and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the owner-creation flow while keeping the flat `address` fully backward-compatible.\n\n**API contract (`openapi.yml`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to `OwnerFields` (so they also appear on the `Owner` response via `allOf`).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only request isn't rejected by Bean Validation; the \"either form, plus city\" rule is enforced in code where it can return the proper error.\n\n**Persistence**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields (with `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns) to the `Owner` entity, with documented getters/setters.\n- Added the two columns to all four schema dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n**Address logic (each piece in its own place)**\n- `AddressNormalizer.compose(line1, line2)` owns the composition rule: normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when line 2 is present. The existing per-field `normalize` is reused.\n- `OwnerFieldsValidator` now treats the owner as valid when a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** flat `address` is supplied (reporting `addressLine1` when neither is), factoring the blank check into a shared helper.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeAddress(...)` prefers the structured form: when `addressLine1` is non-blank it normalizes both lines and stores the composed value into `address`; otherwise it normalizes the flat `address`. Storing the resolved value into the single `address` field means everything downstream (household hash, postcode validation, locality \u2014 all keyed on `postcode`/`city`) reads the structured-preferred address consistently.\n- The MapStruct `OwnerMapper` picks up the new fields automatically by name (verified in the generated impl).\n\n**Verification:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS. This covers both the new structured normalize/compose test and the flat-address backward-compatibility test.",
